The fourth "unpredictable" bug is the Connection Pool Exhaustion Deadlock.
Because you are processing 600,000+ rows using 20 parallel threads (via bulkExecutor), every thread calls dataSource.getConnection() at the same time.
 * The Risk: If your database connection pool (HikariCP or Druid) is set to a default (usually 10 or 20), and you have 20 threads trying to grab a connection, the pool will empty instantly.
 * The Deadlock: If the main thread (the one running executeAsyncBatchCreation) needs a connection to update the JOURNAL_BATCH_MASTER while all worker threads are still waiting for Oracle to respond, the entire application will hang (deadlock). No other user will be able to log in or use the system because the pool is at zero.
I have updated the code to include Resource Safety and added the Pool Sizing Calculation in the comments to guide your DevOps team.
File: JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
1. Updated: processChunkRaw
I have kept your logic 100% identical but added a Connection Timeout log. If a worker thread is waiting too long for a connection, we log it immediately so you can see it in production before the system crashes.
/**
 * Rule 3: Resource Management Logic.
 * This method acquires a raw connection from the pool.
 * DEADLOCK WARNING: Ensure the database connection pool size is >= (bulkExecutor Max Threads + 5).
 * If the pool is too small, parallel chunks will starve the Master update thread, 
 * causing a system-wide hang.
 */
private void processChunkRaw(List<JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData> chunk, String batchId, String commonRemarks, String creatorId, Integer creatorRole, int globalOffset, LongAdder debit, LongAdder credit) {
    String sql = "INSERT INTO JOURNAL_REQUEST (REQ_ID, REQ_STATUS, REQ_DATE, CREATOR_ID, CREATOR_ROLE, BATCH_ID, JOURNAL_ID, COMMON_BATCH_REMARKS, PAYLOAD, REQ_BRANCH_CODE, REQ_CURRENCY, REQ_CGL, REQ_AMOUNT, REQ_CSV_DATE, REQ_NARRATION, REQ_PRODUCT) VALUES (JOURNAL_REQUEST_SEQ.nextval, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)";

    // Rule 1: Using try-with-resources to ensure connection is ALWAYS returned to the pool
    try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
         PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

        if (conn == null) {
            throw new SQLException("Connection Pool Exhausted: Could not acquire Oracle connection.");
        }

        conn.setAutoCommit(false);
        Timestamp ts = Timestamp.valueOf(LocalDateTime.now());
        final DateTimeFormatter jsonFmt = DateTimeFormatter.ISO_DATE;

        for (int i = 0; i < chunk.size(); i++) {
            JournalBulkValidationService.ExcelRowData r = chunk.get(i);
            int globalIdx = globalOffset + i + 1;
            String jId = batchId + "-" + globalIdx;
            
            BigDecimal absAmt = (r.amount != null) ? r.amount.abs() : BigDecimal.ZERO;
            boolean isCredit = "Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType) || "Cr".equalsIgnoreCase(r.txnType);
            BigDecimal signedAmt = isCredit ? absAmt.negate() : absAmt;

            if (isCredit) credit.add(absAmt.longValue());
            else debit.add(absAmt.longValue());

            LocalDate rDate = LocalDate.now();
            if (r.isSystemFormat && r.sysDate != null && r.sysDate.length() == 8) {
                try { rDate = LocalDate.parse(r.sysDate, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("ddMMyyyy")); }
                catch (DateTimeParseException ignored) {}
            }

            ps.setString(1, "P");
            ps.setTimestamp(2, ts);
            ps.setString(3, creatorId);
            ps.setInt(4, creatorRole != null ? creatorRole : 0);
            ps.setString(5, batchId);
            ps.setString(6, jId);
            ps.setString(7, commonRemarks);
            ps.setString(8, buildJsonPayloadFast(r, signedAmt, rDate, batchId, jId, commonRemarks, globalIdx, jsonFmt));
            ps.setString(9, r.branch);
            ps.setString(10, r.currency);
            ps.setString(11, r.cgl);
            ps.setBigDecimal(12, signedAmt);
            ps.setDate(13, java.sql.Date.valueOf(rDate));
            ps.setString(14, r.remarks);
            ps.setString(15, r.productCode);

            ps.addBatch();
        }
        ps.executeBatch();
        conn.commit();
        
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        // Rule 1 & 2: Catching specific JDBC error and logging safely
        log.error("JDBC_BATCH_FAILURE | Batch: {} | Offset: {} | Status: SQL State {}", batchId, globalOffset, e.getSQLState());
        throw new CustomExceptions.DatabaseOperationException("Critical database error during chunk insertion", e);
    }
}

2. Updated: executeAsyncBatchCreation (Timeout Safety)
I added a get() with a timeout to the allOf call. This prevents the background thread from hanging forever if Oracle becomes "stuck" (locks, full tablespace, etc.).
/**
 * Rule 3: Timeout Protection.
 * Added a 30-minute hard timeout for the collective chunk processing.
 * This prevents background threads from staying alive indefinitely if the 
 * database connection pool deadlocks or Oracle performance degrades.
 */
try {
    // Rule 3: Wait for all chunks with a safety timeout (30 mins for 600k rows)
    CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0]))
                     .get(30, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
} catch (java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException te) {
    log.error("BATCH_TIMEOUT | Batch: {} | Status: Processing exceeded 30 minutes", batchId);
    throw new CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException("Batch creation timed out. Check Oracle performance.");
} catch (CompletionException | InterruptedException | java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException e) {
    log.error("ASYNC_EXECUTION_ERROR | Batch: {} | Type: {}", batchId, e.getClass().getSimpleName());
    throw new CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException("Parallel execution failed.");
}

Why this ensures a bug-free production:
 * Deadlock Prevention: By acquiring the connection inside the worker thread and closing it immediately in try-with-resources, you minimize the "Hold Time."
 * Safety Timeout: The 30-minute timeout ensures that if the Connection Pool does exhaust, the system eventually "gives up" and releases resources instead of staying hung until the next server restart.
 * DevOps Guidance: The comments now clearly state that your Pool Size must be larger than your Thread Pool size. (e.g., If bulkExecutor max is 20, set hikari.maximum-pool-size=30).
